---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/pricing';
const title = 'Shopify SMS pricing: model the invoice and incremental contribution';
const desc = 'A practical SMS cost worksheet covering units, platform charges, peak volume, discounts, and the limits of attributed revenue.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/guides">All guides</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Campaign economics</p>
    <h1>Price the SMS program before comparing the revenue dashboard</h1>
    <p>Build two separate worksheets: one predicts the cash invoice; the other evaluates whether the program adds contribution after operating costs. Revenue per send, contribution per send, and return on investment are different measures. This guide uses hypothetical arithmetic, not merchant results.</p>
    <h2>Collect inputs that match billing</h2>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>What to record</th><th>Why it changes the model</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Audience</td><td>Eligible subscribers by destination market and channel</td><td>Total contacts need not equal billable profiles or SMS recipients</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Traffic</td><td>Campaigns plus welcome, recovery, and other automated messages</td><td>Automation continues outside the broadcast calendar</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Units</td><td>Provider-previewed billable units for actual copy and media</td><td>One recipient need not equal one charged unit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plan</td><td>Subscription, minimum, included usage, add-ons, and overages</td><td>Lower unit rates can come with higher fixed cost</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Other charges</td><td>Carrier, sender, setup, services, taxes, and currency where applicable</td><td>The advertised rate may not represent the invoice</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Ask the provider to explain encoding and segmentation for your exact message, including links, personalization, and media. Do not price from a universal character count. Preserve the quote date, destination assumptions, and rate version so a later invoice can be reconciled to the same inputs.</p>
    <h2>A bounded public-rate example</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026, <a href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript's pricing page</a> lists Starter at $0 monthly with a $49 monthly minimum spend and $0.009 per SMS, and Growth at $100 monthly with $0.008 per SMS; carrier fees are additional. The page scopes displayed rates to US 50 states/Canada. Confirm applicability and minimum treatment for your own account.</p>
    <p>For 10,000 billable SMS units, the displayed usage rates give $90 on Starter and $80 on Growth. Adding the displayed Growth subscription gives $180. These are partial calculations before carrier charges, extras, taxes, and contract conditions. They show why a lower usage rate alone does not establish a lower bill.</p>
    <p>In a deliberately simplified model excluding those other differences, Growth's $100 fixed fee divided by the $0.001 usage-rate difference gives 100,000 units as the equality point. At that volume both simplified totals are $900. This is not a recommendation to upgrade: feature needs, minimums, carrier treatment, message mix, and negotiated terms can change the actual decision.</p>
    <h2>Quotes and bundles need different inputs</h2>
    <p><a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">Attentive's pricing page</a> describes pricing around messages, subscriber count, channels, and selected tools. Request an itemized proposal for the same forecast rather than inventing a comparable unit rate. Ask how bundled support and optional products affect renewals and minimum commitments.</p>
    <p>For <a href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo</a>, preserve the calculator inputs and selected products with the quote. A list-size scenario and a message-volume scenario answer different questions. Compare the bill for the entire retained stack, including email you continue paying for; do not count a tool as replaced until its required workflows and data are covered.</p>
    <h2>Stress the forecast</h2>
    <p>Use a normal month, your busiest plausible launch month, and a larger-list month. Keep recipients and units separate: a hypothetical 5,000 recipients receiving two campaigns at one billable unit each produces 10,000 units; at two units per message it produces 20,000. Add automated traffic to both scenarios.</p>
    <p>Track what happens when a subscriber joins near month end, a plan limit is exceeded, a message is retried, or credits remain unused. Ask which events are charged. Include one-time migration and training costs separately from recurring fees so the first year and a steady month remain comparable.</p>
    <h2>Contribution requires a counterfactual</h2>
    <p>A platform may attribute an order after a message without showing that the message caused it. Where feasible, randomly reserve an eligible group that does not receive the tested marketing send and compare outcomes over the same window. Keep necessary transactional communications and the experimental policy explicit. Report sample size and uncertainty, especially for small or inventory-limited campaigns.</p>
    <p>For illustration only, suppose analysis estimates $1,200 incremental net sales and a 50% contribution rate after variable product and fulfillment costs. That gives $600 before program costs. If messaging costs $150 and incremental support and other program work cost $100, the remainder is $350. This assumes discounts and returns are already reflected in net sales and the contribution rate; subtracting them again would double count.</p>
    <p>Allocate fixed program costs consistently when reporting campaign contribution. Do not subtract the entire monthly subscription from every campaign and then sum the campaigns. If you report ROI, state the cost denominator and allocation method rather than using contribution per send as a synonym.</p>
    <h2>Reconcile after the first invoice</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Match billed units and destination mix to the send records, not only delivered counts.</li>
      <li>Explain each difference between forecast and invoice: audience changes, segments, automations, services, or overages.</li>
      <li>Update the peak forecast and compare it with the current plan and one alternative.</li>
      <li>Review offer cost and combined channel exposure before increasing send frequency to cover fixed fees.</li>
      <li>Keep a decision record with the quote, assumptions, invoice, and remaining uncertainties.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Use <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> for a proposal comparison and <a href="/apps/klaviyo">the Klaviyo evaluation</a> for a shared-profile trial. The lowest modeled invoice is useful only if the workflow meets your audience, suppression, and operating requirements.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
